WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.955 --> 00:00:04.644
♫ Let's all go to Gullah Gullah Island

00:00:04.644 --> 00:00:05.542
♫ Gullah Gullah

00:00:05.542 --> 00:00:06.375
♫ Gullah Gullah

00:00:06.375 --> 00:00:08.189
- [Narrator] Remember
Gullah Gullah Island,

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:10.142
the ‘90s kids show on Nick Jr.

00:00:10.142 --> 00:00:14.050
featuring a fun family and a
polliwog named Binyah Binyah?

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:15.250
(fun music)

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:18.000
Well, it's a real place, kind of.

00:00:20.479 --> 00:00:21.580
- [Natalie] I'm Natalie Daise,

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:23.740
and I played Ms. Natalie
on Gullah Gullah Island.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:26.584
- [Ron] My name is Ron Daise,
and I served as the dad.

00:00:26.584 --> 00:00:28.069
(clicking)
- Show me how old

00:00:28.069 --> 00:00:29.766
you are Shaina.
- Five.

00:00:29.766 --> 00:00:32.790
- [Ron] There is no real place
named Gullah Gullah Island,

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:33.873
except on TV.

00:00:35.184 --> 00:00:38.959
It is based on the Gullah Geechee culture,

00:00:38.959 --> 00:00:43.493
which was passed on by
enslaved West Africans

00:00:43.493 --> 00:00:46.243
who were brought to this country.

00:00:47.449 --> 00:00:49.040
- [Narrator] Gullah Geechee
culture was born out of

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:50.958
several African cultures and languages

00:00:50.958 --> 00:00:53.270
brought here by enslaved people.

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:55.702
Everything from cuisines
and traditional practices

00:00:55.702 --> 00:00:58.313
have been maintained through
generations of settlement

00:00:58.313 --> 00:01:01.792
in coastal villages in
Georgia and the Carolinas.

00:01:01.792 --> 00:01:03.952
- I am Gullah Geechee and proud of it.

00:01:03.952 --> 00:01:05.899
It's important to me to make sure that

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:08.658
Gullah Geechee culture is preserved,

00:01:08.658 --> 00:01:11.658
because it's a very important factor

00:01:12.784 --> 00:01:15.284
of American cultural heritage.

00:01:17.848 --> 00:01:19.566
- [Narrator] When Natalie
and Ron got the opportunity

00:01:19.566 --> 00:01:22.963
to present their culture on
TV, they jumped at the chance.

00:01:22.963 --> 00:01:24.772
- [Natalie] By the time we
met the woman who became

00:01:24.772 --> 00:01:26.639
the executive producer,
we had been traveling

00:01:26.639 --> 00:01:28.516
around the country doing stories and songs

00:01:28.516 --> 00:01:29.707
of Gullah culture.

00:01:29.707 --> 00:01:31.207
That's what we did.

00:01:31.207 --> 00:01:32.833
In the beginning we
didn't know that it was so

00:01:32.833 --> 00:01:35.377
significant; there had never
been a show like that before.

00:01:35.377 --> 00:01:37.973
We wanted a show where our
children could see themselves.

00:01:37.973 --> 00:01:40.183
We actually ended up with a show where

00:01:40.183 --> 00:01:43.857
everybody could see themselves in us.

00:01:43.857 --> 00:01:45.710
- [Narrator] Before we
go, would you guys mind

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:46.682
singing us out?

00:01:46.682 --> 00:01:48.014
(laughing)

00:01:48.014 --> 00:01:51.050
- [Natalie] Man, we get asked that a lot.

00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:53.561
♫ Come and let's play together

00:01:53.561 --> 00:01:56.536
♫ In the bright sunny weather

00:01:56.536 --> 00:02:00.619
♫ Let's all go to Gullah Gullah Island

00:02:00.619 --> 00:02:03.095
♫ Gullah Gullah Island

00:02:03.095 --> 00:02:06.428
♫ Gullah Gullah Island

